course: dictionary
lesson: 931
title: Blindfold and the senses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A blindfold is a cloth tied over someone's eyes so they cannot see. The word has three forms you will meet: a blindfold (a noun, the cloth), to blindfold (a verb, to put the cloth on), and blindfold or blindfolded (doing something without seeing). Look for the clues around the word to decide which one it is.
explain: «Peeking under the blindfold in blind man's buff is soon seen.» Here the blindfold is the cloth, and the clue is peeking. If you peek, you can see, so the blindfold has stopped you seeing. Try your meaning in the sentence: "peeking under the cloth over the eyes" still makes sense.
explain: «Stepping out there is like stepping out blindfolded.» Blindfolded is used here like an adjective meaning without seeing. The clue is stepping out: you move forward but cannot see where your feet are going. The word does not mean wearing a blindfold in the sentence; it means the feeling of not being able to see.
fact: A blindfold is a cloth tied over someone's eyes so they cannot see.
fact: To blindfold someone is to put such a cloth over their eyes.
fact: Doing something blindfold means doing it without seeing.
fact: «A seal's whiskers feel tiny movements in the water.» Seals use touch, not only sight, to find things.
fact: Blindfolds are used in party games like blind man's buff and pin the tail on the donkey, and in science tests such as tasting with a blindfold.
exercise: «Peeking under the blindfold in blind man's buff is soon seen.» What is a blindfold? | *A cloth tied over someone's eyes so they cannot see | A game where you run and hide | A kind of hat for the sun | A rope used to tie up a boat
exercise: Mum will blindfold you for the taste test. What does blindfold mean as a verb here? | To take off your shoes before you eat | To shout the answer out loud | *To put a cloth over someone's eyes so they cannot see | To count the points at the end
exercise: «Stepping out there is like stepping out blindfolded.» What does blindfolded suggest? | *Without being able to see where you are going | Being with your best friend | Wearing a hat that is too big | Going very fast down a hill
test: In an experiment, «a blindfolded seal followed the trail of a fish model seconds after it passed.» What does blindfolded mean here? | *Unable to see, so the seal used its whiskers to find the trail | Very hungry | Wearing a warm coat | Swimming very fast
test: Ravi wore a blindfold for the taste test and guessed each drink by smell and taste. Why did he wear a blindfold? | To keep the sun out of his eyes | *So he could not see which drink he was tasting | To keep his hair tidy | To look cool in front of his friends
exam: Which word names the cloth itself? | *a blindfold | to blindfold | blindfolding | blindfolded
